49
00:03:26,930 --> 00:03:30,449
the demonstrator's goals might be. Self
driving cars can

50
00:03:30,449 --> 00:03:34,050
serve as A simplistic illustration of how
behavioral cloning

51
00:03:34,050 --> 00:03:38,330
operates A human demonstrator driver is
directed to operate

52
00:03:38,330 --> 00:03:41,449
a car during which data about the
environment state

53
00:03:41,870 --> 00:03:44,789
from sensors like LIDAR and cameras, along
with the

54
00:03:44,789 --> 00:03:48,909
actions taken by the demonstrator are
collected. These actions

55
00:03:48,909 --> 00:03:52,949
can include wheel movements, gear use,
etc. This creates

56
00:03:52,949 --> 00:03:58,110
a data set comprising state action pairs.
Subsequently, supervised

57
00:03:58,110 --> 00:04:01,310
learning is used to train a prediction
model which

58
00:04:01,310 --> 00:04:04,110
attempts to predict an action for any
future environment

59
00:04:04,349 --> 00:04:08,069
state. For instance, the model might
output a specific

60
00:04:08,069 --> 00:04:11,349
steering wheel and gear configuration
based on the camera

61
00:04:11,349 --> 00:04:15,000
feed. When the model achieves sufficient
accuracy, it can

62
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,600
be stated that the human driver's behavior
has been

63
00:04:17,600 --> 00:04:21,279
cloned into a machine via learning, hence
the term

64
00:04:21,279 --> 00:04:25,360
behavioral cloning. The following points
highlight several potential issues

65
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:31,040
that might surface when employing
behavioral one. Confident in

66
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,160
during the demonstrations, the human
experts have some amount

67
00:04:35,160 --> 00:04:37,870
of background knowledge that they rely on
which is

68
00:04:37,870 --> 00:04:40,750
not taught to the model. For example, when
training

69
00:04:40,750 --> 00:04:44,670
an LLM to have conversations using
behavioral cloning, the

70
00:04:44,670 --> 00:04:48,350
human demonstrator might less frequently
ask certain questions because

71
00:04:48,350 --> 00:04:51,709
they are considered common sense. A model
trained to

72
00:04:51,709 --> 00:04:55,189
imitate will copy both the types of
questions asked

73
00:04:55,189 --> 00:04:58,389
in conversation as well as the frequency
with which

74
00:04:58,389 --> 00:05:02,389
they are asked. Humans already possess
this background knowledge,

75
00:05:02,470 --> 00:05:05,750
but an LLM doesn't. This means that to
have

76
00:05:05,750 --> 00:05:08,230
the same level of information as a human,
the

77
00:05:08,230 --> 00:05:11,029
model should ask some questions more
frequently to fill

78
00:05:11,029 --> 00:05:13,709
the gaps in its knowledge. But since the
model

79
00:05:13,709 --> 00:05:15,870
seeks to imitate, it will stick to the low

80
00:05:15,870 --> 00:05:19,310
frequency demonstrated by the human and
thus has strictly

81
00:05:19,310 --> 00:05:22,790
less information overall than the
demonstrator for the same

82
00:05:22,790 --> 00:05:26,990
conversational task. Despite this dearth
of knowledge, we expect

83
00:05:26,990 --> 00:05:28,949
it to be able to perform as a clone

84
00:05:28,949 --> 00:05:32,389
and reach human level performance. This
means in order

85
00:05:32,389 --> 00:05:35,430
to reach human performance on less than
human knowledge,

86
00:05:35,430 --> 00:05:38,029
it will resort to making up facts that
help

87
00:05:38,029 --> 00:05:41,990
it reach its performance goals. These
hallucinations will then

88
00:05:41,990 --> 00:05:44,709
be presented during the conversation with
the same level

89
00:05:44,709 --> 00:05:48,350
of confidence as all the other
information. Hallucinations and

90
00:05:48,350 --> 00:05:52,069
confident incorrectness is an empirically
verified problem in many

91
00:05:52,069 --> 00:05:56,430
LLMs including GPT 2 and 3, and raises
obvious

92
00:05:56,430 --> 00:06:03,180
concerns for AI safety. Xiao et al. 2021
Underachieving

93
00:06:03,339 --> 00:06:06,819
the types of hallucinations mentioned
above arose because the

94
00:06:06,819 --> 00:06:10,060
model knew too little. However, the model
can also

95
00:06:10,060 --> 00:06:12,899
know too much. If the model knows more
than

96
00:06:12,899 --> 00:06:15,579
the human demonstrator because it is able
to find

97
00:06:15,579 --> 00:06:17,860
more patterns in the environment state
that it is

98
00:06:17,860 --> 00:06:21,339
given, it will throw away that information
and reduce

99
00:06:21,339 --> 00:06:24,670
its performance to match human level. This
is because

100
00:06:24,670 --> 00:06:27,990
it is trained as a clone. Ideally, we
don't

101
00:06:27,990 --> 00:06:30,949
want the model dumbing itself down or not
disclosing

102
00:06:30,949 --> 00:06:33,910
useful new patterns in data just because
it is

103
00:06:33,910 --> 00:06:35,949
trying to be human like or perform at a

104
00:06:35,949 --> 00:06:38,990
human level. This is another problem that
will have

105
00:06:38,990 --> 00:06:41,949
to be addressed if behavioral cloning
continues to be

106
00:06:41,949 --> 00:06:47,470
used as an ML technique. Procedural
Cloning we define

107
00:06:47,470 --> 00:06:53,569
procedure cloning as follows. Procedure
cloning extends behavioral cloning

108
00:06:54,850 --> 00:06:58,370
by not just imitating the demonstrator's
outputs, but also

109
00:06:58,370 --> 00:07:02,769
imitating the complete sequence of
intermediate computations associated with

110
00:07:02,769 --> 00:07:06,410
an expert's procedure. In bc, the agent
learns to

111
00:07:06,410 --> 00:07:09,930
map states directly to actions by
discarding the intermediate

112
00:07:09,930 --> 00:07:13,129
search outputs. On the other hand, the PC
approach

113
00:07:13,129 --> 00:07:17,439
learns the entire sequence of intermediate
computations, including branches

114
00:07:17,439 --> 00:07:21,800
and backtracks during training. During
inference, PC generates a

115
00:07:21,800 --> 00:07:25,160
sequence of intermediate search outcomes
that mimic the expert

116
00:07:25,160 --> 00:07:28,720
search procedure before outputting the
final action. The main

117
00:07:28,720 --> 00:07:31,439
difference between PC and BC lies in the
information

118
00:07:31,519 --> 00:07:35,480
they utilize. BC only has access to expert
state

119
00:07:35,480 --> 00:07:39,240
action pairs as demonstrations, while PC
also has access

120
00:07:39,240 --> 00:07:42,800
to the intermediate computations that
generated those state action

121
00:07:42,800 --> 00:07:46,199
pairs. PC learns to predict the complete
series of

122
00:07:46,199 --> 00:07:50,199
intermediate computation outcomes,
enabling it to generalize better to

123
00:07:50,199 --> 00:07:54,079
test environments with different
configurations compared to alternative
improvements

124
00:07:54,079 --> 00:07:58,079
over BC. PC's ability to imitate the
expert search

125
00:07:58,079 --> 00:08:01,160
procedure allows it to capture the
underlying reasoning and

126
00:08:01,160 --> 00:08:04,839
decision making process, leading to
improved performance in various

127
00:08:04,839 --> 00:08:08,720
tasks. A limitation of PC is the
computational overhead

128
00:08:08,720 --> 00:08:12,310
compared to bc, as PC needs to predict
intermediate

129
00:08:12,310 --> 00:08:15,750
procedures. Additionally, the choice of
how to encode the

130
00:08:15,750 --> 00:08:18,949
expert's algorithm into a form suitable
for PC is

131
00:08:18,949 --> 00:08:21,589
left to the practitioner, which may
require some trial

132
00:08:21,589 --> 00:08:26,589
and error in designing the ideal
computation sequence. Inverse

133
00:08:26,589 --> 00:08:33,110
Reinforcement Learning the term inverse
reinforcement learning refers to

134
00:08:33,110 --> 00:08:38,629
the Inverse reinforcement learning
represents a form of machine

135
00:08:38,629 --> 00:08:42,350
learning wherein an artificial
intelligence observes the behavior of

136
00:08:42,350 --> 00:08:46,190
another agent within a particular
environment, typically an expert

137
00:08:46,190 --> 00:08:49,669
human, and endeavors to discern the reward
function without

138
00:08:49,669 --> 00:08:53,469
its explicit definition. IRL is typically
employed when a

139
00:08:53,469 --> 00:08:57,470
reward function is too intricate to define
programmatically or

140
00:08:57,470 --> 00:09:00,470
when AI agents need to react robustly to
sudden

141
00:09:00,470 --> 00:09:05,029
environmental changes necessitating a
modification in the reward function

142
00:09:05,029 --> 00:09:08,350
for safety. For instance, consider an AI
agent learning

143
00:09:08,350 --> 00:09:12,590
to execute a backflip. Humans, dogs, and
Boston Dynamics

144
00:09:12,590 --> 00:09:15,590
robots can all perform backflips, but the
manner in

145
00:09:15,590 --> 00:09:18,509
which they do so varies significantly
depending on their

146
00:09:18,509 --> 00:09:23,029
physiology, their incentives, and their
current location, all of

147
00:09:23,029 --> 00:09:25,309
which can be highly diverse in the real
world.

148
00:09:25,710 --> 00:09:29,029
An AI agent learning backflips purely
through trial and

149
00:09:29,029 --> 00:09:31,629
error across a wide range of body types
and

150
00:09:31,629 --> 00:09:35,779
locations without something to observe
might prove highly inefficient.

151
00:09:36,019 --> 00:09:39,620
Irl, therefore, does not necessarily imply
that an AI

152
00:09:39,620 --> 00:09:43,820
mimics other agents behavior, since AI
researchers may anticipate

153
00:09:43,820 --> 00:09:46,539
the AI agent to devise more efficient ways
to

154
00:09:46,539 --> 00:09:51,340
maximize the discovered reward function.
Nevertheless, IRL does assume

155
00:09:51,340 --> 00:09:54,539
that the observed agent behaves
transparently enough for an

156
00:09:54,539 --> 00:09:58,019
AI agent to accurately identify their
actions and what

157
00:09:58,019 --> 00:10:02,460
success constitutes. This means that IRL
endeavours to discover

158
00:10:02,460 --> 00:10:06,899
the reward functions that explain the
demonstrations. This should

159
00:10:06,899 --> 00:10:10,139
not be conflated with imitation learning,
where the primary

160
00:10:10,139 --> 00:10:13,299
interest is a policy capable of generating
the observed

161
00:10:13,299 --> 00:10:18,539
demonstrations. The textbook includes
Figure 6.11. Here it depicts

162
00:10:18,539 --> 00:10:21,419
a simple illustration of the flow
difference between RL

163
00:10:21,419 --> 00:10:25,379
and irl. IRL constitutes both a machine
learning method

164
00:10:25,379 --> 00:10:27,639
and, since it can be employed when
specifying a

165
00:10:27,639 --> 00:10:31,200
reward function, is excessively
challenging and a machine learning

166
00:10:31,200 --> 00:10:33,919
problem as an AI agent may settle on an

167
00:10:33,919 --> 00:10:38,200
inaccurate reward function or utilize
unsafe and misaligned methods

168
00:10:38,200 --> 00:10:40,639
to achieve it. One of the limitations to
this

169
00:10:40,639 --> 00:10:44,159
approach is that IRL algorithms presume
that the observed

170
00:10:44,159 --> 00:10:48,159
behavior is optimal, an assumption that
arguably proves too

171
00:10:48,159 --> 00:10:52,529
robust when dealing with human
demonstrations. Another problem is

172
00:10:52,529 --> 00:10:55,169
that the IRL problem is ill posed as every

173
00:10:55,169 --> 00:10:58,490
policy is optimal for the null reward. For
most

174
00:10:58,490 --> 00:11:03,649
behavioral observations, multiple fitting
reward functions exist. This set

175
00:11:03,649 --> 00:11:07,249
of solutions often includes many
degenerate solutions which assign

176
00:11:07,249 --> 00:11:12,490
zero rewards to all states. Cooperative
Inverse Reinforcement Learning

177
00:11:14,889 --> 00:11:18,490
Cooperative inverse reinforcement learning
is an extension of the

178
00:11:18,490 --> 00:11:24,889
irl. IRL is a learning approach that aims
to

179
00:11:24,889 --> 00:11:28,289
infer the underlying reward function of an
expert by

180
00:11:28,289 --> 00:11:31,929
observing their behavior. It assumes that
the expert's behavior

181
00:11:31,929 --> 00:11:34,730
is optimal and tries to learn a reward
function

182
00:11:34,730 --> 00:11:38,690
that explains their actions. Crrl, on the
other hand,

183
00:11:38,850 --> 00:11:42,049
is an interactive form of IRL that
addresses two

184
00:11:42,049 --> 00:11:46,149
major weaknesses of conventional irl.
First, instead of simply

185
00:11:46,149 --> 00:11:49,750
copying the human reward function, CRL is
formulated as

186
00:11:49,750 --> 00:11:53,870
a learning process. It is an interactive
reward maximization

187
00:11:53,950 --> 00:11:56,990
process where the human functions as a
teacher and

188
00:11:56,990 --> 00:11:59,830
provides feedback in the form of rewards
on the

189
00:11:59,830 --> 00:12:03,190
agent's actions. This allows the human to
nudge the

190
00:12:03,190 --> 00:12:06,870
AI agent towards behavioral patterns that
align with their

191
00:12:06,870 --> 00:12:11,009
preferences. The second weakness of
conventional IRL is is

192
00:12:11,009 --> 00:12:14,129
that it assumes the human behaves
optimally, which limits

193
00:12:14,129 --> 00:12:17,929
the teaching behaviors that can be
considered. CARL addresses

194
00:12:17,929 --> 00:12:20,730
this weakness by allowing for a variety of
teaching

195
00:12:20,730 --> 00:12:24,049
behaviors and interactions between the
human and the AI

196
00:12:24,049 --> 00:12:27,250
agent. It enables the AI agent to learn
not

197
00:12:27,250 --> 00:12:29,769
only what actions to take, but also how
and

198
00:12:29,769 --> 00:12:32,649
why to take them by observing and
interacting with

199
00:12:32,649 --> 00:12:35,370
the human. Searle has been studied as a
potential

200
00:12:35,370 --> 00:12:39,480
approach to AI alignment, particularly in
scenarios where deep

201
00:12:39,480 --> 00:12:43,279
learning may not scale to AGI. However,
opinions on

202
00:12:43,279 --> 00:12:47,240
the potential effectiveness of CIRL vary,
with some researchers

203
00:12:47,240 --> 00:12:49,919
expecting it to be helpful if deep
learning doesn't

204
00:12:49,919 --> 00:12:52,879
scale to AGI while others have a higher
probability

205
00:12:52,879 --> 00:12:57,039
of deep learning. Scaling to AGI the Goal
Inference

206
00:12:57,039 --> 00:13:00,440
Problem the term goal inference problem
refers to the

207
00:13:01,159 --> 00:13:03,679
the goal inference problem refers to the
task of

208
00:13:03,679 --> 00:13:06,700
inferring the goals or intentions of an
agent based

209
00:13:06,700 --> 00:13:10,220
on their observed behavior or actions.
This final section

210
00:13:10,220 --> 00:13:13,740
builds upon the limitations highlighted in
previous sections to

211
00:13:13,740 --> 00:13:17,460
introduce the goal inference problem and
its simpler subset,

212
00:13:17,539 --> 00:13:22,179
the easy goal inference problem. Imitation
learning based approaches

213
00:13:22,340 --> 00:13:27,220
generally follows these Observe the user's
actions and statements.

214
00:13:27,700 --> 00:13:31,620
Deduce the user's preferences. Endeavor to
enhance the world

215
00:13:31,700 --> 00:13:35,409
according to the user's preferences,
possibly collaborating with the

216
00:13:35,409 --> 00:13:38,929
user and seeking clarification as needed.
The merit of

217
00:13:38,929 --> 00:13:42,009
this method is that we can immediately
start constructing

218
00:13:42,009 --> 00:13:45,850
systems that are driven by observed user
behavior. However,

219
00:13:45,929 --> 00:13:48,610
as a consequence of this approach, we run
into

220
00:13:48,610 --> 00:13:52,169
the goal inference problem. This refers to
the task

221
00:13:52,169 --> 00:13:55,289
of inferring the goals or intentions of an
agent

222
00:13:55,450 --> 00:13:59,169
based on their observed behavior or
actions. It involves

223
00:13:59,169 --> 00:14:02,110
determining what the agent is trying to
achieve or

224
00:14:02,110 --> 00:14:05,750
what their desired outcome is. The goal
inference problem

225
00:14:05,750 --> 00:14:09,629
is challenging because agents may act
suboptimally or fail

226
00:14:09,629 --> 00:14:12,950
to achieve their goals, making it
difficult to accurately

227
00:14:12,950 --> 00:14:17,509
infer their true intentions. Traditional
approaches to goal inference

228
00:14:17,590 --> 00:14:21,350
often assume that agents act optimally or
exhibit simplified

229
00:14:21,429 --> 00:14:24,669
forms of sub optimality which may not
capture the

230
00:14:24,669 --> 00:14:28,629
complexity of real world planning and
decision making. Therefore,

231
00:14:28,629 --> 00:14:32,110
the goal inference problem requires
accounting for the difficulty

232
00:14:32,110 --> 00:14:35,350
of planning itself and the possibility of
